title: Drill — delete up to the match
chapter: 12
type: drill
skills: search-motion
par: 18
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: click, shift-click, Delete
open: summarise.ts

@brief
Eight times: the four debug lines between the opening brace and the
`return` go. One operator, one search, no counting.

@keys
| `d/text` `Enter` | delete from the cursor up to the next match |
| `^` | first non-blank character of the line |

@hints
Get onto the first line of the junk — first non-blank character, not
column zero — and delete up to the `return`.
--
`j^` puts you there. Then `d/return` `Enter`. Starting at the first
non-blank means the indentation in front of the cursor survives and the
`return` keeps its two spaces.

@solution
j  ^  d/return<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes. Four of the eight reps should be enough for the
sequence to stop feeling like three decisions and start feeling like one.

The `^` is not a detail you can skip. Deleting from column zero would
take the indentation with it and leave the `return` jammed against the
margin. `d` starts where the cursor is, so where the cursor is *within*
the line matters as much as which line it is on.

@start summarise.ts
export function summarise(orders: Order[]) {
  const started = Date.now();
  console.log('[summarise] orders in', orders.length);
  console.table(orders.map((order) => order.status));
  console.log('[summarise] elapsed ms', Date.now() - started);
  return orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'PENDING');
}

@expected summarise.ts
export function summarise(orders: Order[]) {
  return orders.filter((order) => order.status === 'PENDING');
}
